  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSW flow ahead of a larger mid level trough is in place across the \nNorthern Plains, with a PV height anomaly associated with a mid \nlevel shortwave ejecting into eastern ND within this flow. Higher \nlevel clouds in SD to the south of this PV anomaly could be the next \narea of forcing that could trigger initiation along the frontal zone \nin the 22-00Z period. The frontal zone is currently in place from \nLake of the Woods to just south of Grand Forks and just west of \nFargo and is starting to show signs of moving east-southeast on \nradar/satellite ahead of this mid level wave. Good forcing for \nascent and at least elevated instability will continue to support \nshowers and thunderstorms (some severe) into the evening before \ndry/stable air arrives later this evening/overnight.\n\nThe larger scale trough/upper low becomes a more dominate feature \nfor our region through the week as it stalls and deepens over the \nHudson Bay region and flattens the larger scale ridging to the \nsouth. This results in either weaker NW flow or westerly flow and \nthe stronger storm track mainly remaining in Canada. However, flow \nwill at least periodically allow for some influence of waves \nrounding the mid/upper low and periodic shower/thunderstorm chances. \nThe signal for PoPs is generally washed out by the variability of \nthese features in Canada, and machine learning is not highlighting a \nspecific period for impacts.\n\nAfter cooler than average temperatures Tuesday, this pattern will \ngenerally support temperatures in seasonal ranges for most days into \nthe weekend (+/- 1 standard deviation of normal), and a break from \nthe much hotter temperatures in the 90+ range. \n\n...Severe Thunderstorm Risk Today..\n\nVery high effective shear will be in place with values already in \nthe 50-70kt range across our CWA. As deeper layer shear vectors will \ntend to be parallel or near parallel to the frontal zone there should \nbe a tendency for clustering of thunderstorm activity that develops \nlater this afternoon/evening. Severe mode and coverage will \nultimately come down to timing of initiation and continued \ndestabilization  along/ahead of the frontal zone before sunset. \nShear profiles support supercell development, however discrete mode \nmay be hard to come by outside of the early part of initiation. \nTornado threat will require surface based parcels which for now \nappear hard to come by, however with clearing that is just starting \nto occur in southeast ND/west central MN ahead of the front cap \nerosion could continue and create a window for this threat early in \nthe event with right movers that do develop. More likely scenarios \nfavor elevated cells and at least a window for transient elevated \nsupercell threat which would primarily favor large hail as a threat. \nWhether clusters can grow upscale with strong enough cold pools in \nthis shear profiles. Line normal vectors are going to tend to be \ntowards the northeast (though the critical angle will be a bit \nhigher due to the strong shear environment). \n\nBackward propagation or training potential along the frontal zone \nraises the potential for localized heavy rain threat particularly \nwith PWATs exceeding 1.5\" along the front. As the front should be \nprogressing quickly eastward there shouldn't be more than 1-3hr of \nthis type of training, but this could be enough with higher rats for \nlocalized flash flood potential if this happens over a town/urban \narea where there is poor drainage.",
